## Prerequisites

Before the mail service configuration, the AWS team completed the following:

* Verified the SES domain identity.
* Generated Amazon SES SMTP credentials.
* Shared the SMTP endpoint and authentication credentials.

Example SMTP relay:

```text
email-smtp.<aws-region>.amazonaws.com
```

---

## Implementation

### Install Sendmail

Installed the required mail packages.

```bash
dnf install sendmail sendmail-cf mailx m4 -y
```

---

### Configure Sendmail

Configured Sendmail to use Amazon SES as the outbound SMTP relay.

Updated the Sendmail configuration with:

* Amazon SES SMTP endpoint
* SMTP authentication
* TLS encryption

Configured the smart host.

```text
SMART_HOST(`email-smtp.<aws-region>.amazonaws.com')
```

Enabled SMTP authentication.

```text
FEATURE(`authinfo')
```

Configured the SMTP authentication credentials.

```text
AuthInfo:email-smtp.<aws-region>.amazonaws.com \
"U:SMTP_USERNAME" \
"I:SMTP_USERNAME" \
"P:SMTP_PASSWORD"
```

Generated the updated Sendmail configuration.

```bash
m4 /etc/mail/sendmail.mc > /etc/mail/sendmail.cf
```

Created the authentication database.

```bash
makemap hash /etc/mail/authinfo.db < /etc/mail/authinfo
```

---

### Start the Mail Service

Started and enabled the Sendmail service.

```bash
systemctl enable sendmail
systemctl restart sendmail
```

Verified service status.

```bash
systemctl status sendmail
```

---

### Test Email Delivery

Sent a test email from each server.

```bash
echo "Amazon SES Mail Test" | mail -s "SES SMTP Test" admin@example.com
```

Verified that the email was successfully delivered through Amazon SES.

